00:14:33,729 --> 00:14:38,399
If the effect class is
irreversible, non-compensable

250
00:14:38,409 --> 00:14:41,309
divergence greater than
zero becomes possible,

251
00:14:42,229 --> 00:14:44,909
and the replay candidate
is rejected outright.

252
00:14:45,409 --> 00:14:50,419
The plan then becomes consult
the effect ledger, compensate

253
00:14:50,419 --> 00:14:54,549
the effects that were applied
and are compensable, emit

254
00:14:54,989 --> 00:14:59,819
tombstones before any forward
replay, and replay only inside

255
00:14:59,819 --> 00:15:03,769
the deduplication horizon
per key range, confirming

256
00:15:04,079 --> 00:15:07,179
sync receipts before
full service is restored.

257
00:15:07,859 --> 00:15:09,519
Just slower and correct.

258
00:15:10,019 --> 00:15:13,689
So split state recovery,
the only part of the

259
00:15:13,689 --> 00:15:15,559
infrastructure came back.

260
00:15:16,059 --> 00:15:18,769
So some consumers are
ahead and some are behind.

261
00:15:19,479 --> 00:15:22,209
So the reflex under pressure
is to replay everything,

262
00:15:22,589 --> 00:15:24,169
which amplifies the incident.

263
00:15:24,849 --> 00:15:26,939
The envelope replaces
the reflex with a

264
00:15:26,939 --> 00:15:28,359
per range decision.

265
00:15:28,859 --> 00:15:32,349
Every key ev-- each
key range lands in one

266
00:15:32,349 --> 00:15:33,339
of the four states.

267
00:15:33,839 --> 00:15:37,449
Evidence complete and
every bound within policy.

268
00:15:37,949 --> 00:15:41,269
Resume, execute the selected
plan for that range.

269
00:15:41,769 --> 00:15:45,789
Evidence complete but a
soft bound exceeded is a

270
00:15:45,789 --> 00:15:49,789
case where trade explicitly
compensate, roll back,

271
00:15:49,849 --> 00:15:53,789
or delay and let Pareto
ranking and policy decide

272
00:15:54,059 --> 00:15:55,389
with the decision journaled.

273
00:15:55,889 --> 00:15:59,079
Evidence complete but a
hard bound vi-violated is

274
00:15:59,079 --> 00:16:01,819
a case where you reject
the candidate, stop

275
00:16:01,859 --> 00:16:05,249
irreversible actions,
quarantine the range, and

276
00:16:05,249 --> 00:16:07,509
serve last known good reads.

277
00:16:08,009 --> 00:16:12,119
Evidence unknown is fail
closed, where the bound

278
00:16:12,119 --> 00:16:16,849
expands and the range
stays isolated or read-only

279
00:16:17,109 --> 00:16:18,369
until evidence arrives.

280
00:16:19,079 --> 00:16:21,519
And the only way around
that is a break glass

281
00:16:21,549 --> 00:16:25,239
policy that names its
accepted damage bound.

282
00:16:25,619 --> 00:16:27,969
No judgment calls under
pressure, and every

283
00:16:27,969 --> 00:16:29,279
response is recorded.

284
00:16:29,779 --> 00:16:32,699
So none of this works
if you cannot see it.

285
00:16:33,099 --> 00:16:35,269
So here is what the
planner has to publish

286
00:16:35,299 --> 00:16:36,119
before you need it.

287
00:16:36,619 --> 00:16:39,739
Per-range evidence age
histograms, because a

288
00:16:39,739 --> 00:16:42,309
decision made on stale
proof is a stale decision.

289
00:16:42,809 --> 00:16:46,879
Rejected candidate counts
broken down by constraint, so

290
00:16:46,879 --> 00:16:50,139
you can see which hard limit
is actually doing the work.

291
00:16:51,089 --> 00:16:54,389
The fraction of ranges
operating with unknown

292
00:16:54,389 --> 00:16:56,779
bounds, which is your
failed closed exposure,

293
00:16:57,529 --> 00:17:01,549
and the number your on-call
will care about the most.

294
00:17:02,049 --> 00:17:05,029
So planner latency and
journal durability lag

295
00:17:05,519 --> 00:17:08,369
is another metric because
the decision has to be

296
00:17:08,379 --> 00:17:09,939
durable before it executes.

297
00:17:10,439 --> 00:17:15,279
On cost, the dominance pass
is quadratic in the number

298
00:17:15,279 --> 00:17:18,499
of candidate frontiers
per range and linear in

299
00:17:18,499 --> 00:17:19,619
the number of ranges.

300
00:17:20,479 --> 00:17:23,379
And candidate pruning
by checkpoint lineage

301
00:17:23,609 --> 00:17:27,209
and monotonic frontiers
keeps that small.

302
00:17:27,709 --> 00:17:31,379
The control path stays outside
the high-throughput data path.

303
00:17:31,889 --> 00:17:35,349
Only recovery evidence
and effect identifiers

304
00:17:35,349 --> 00:17:36,039
are retained.

305
00:17:36,389 --> 00:17:40,139
So storage scales with
recovery granularity,

306
00:17:40,419 --> 00:17:42,069
not with event volume.

307
00:17:42,839 --> 00:17:45,809
Exact ledgers are
mandatory only for

308
00:17:45,819 --> 00:17:47,289
non-compensable effects.

309
00:17:47,879 --> 00:17:52,029
Everything else can use
sketches as as long as

310
00:17:52,049 --> 00:17:54,689
the sketch carries an
explicit confidence bound

311
00:17:55,189 --> 00:17:59,869
So the three operational
rules are first, a planner

312
00:17:59,909 --> 00:18:04,999
outage must not revoke
existing ownership or trigger

313
00:18:05,619 --> 00:18:07,369
an unverified promotion.

314
00:18:07,869 --> 00:18:12,639
Current epochs stay valid and
new promotions fail closed.

315
00:18:13,139 --> 00:18:16,539
An outage of the decision
layer never turns into

316
00:18:16,539 --> 00:18:18,039
an unverified failover.

317
00:18:18,539 --> 00:18:20,829
Second, break glass.

318
00:18:21,669 --> 00:18:25,879
Operators need an override
and the model provides one.

319
00:18:26,619 --> 00:18:29,699
But the override is a
separately audited policy

320
00:18:29,969 --> 00:18:32,829
that can be invoked
only when it names the

321
00:18:32,829 --> 00:18:34,549
accepted damage bound.

322
00:18:35,049 --> 00:18:39,379
Emergency access that cannot
state its blast radius is not

323
00:18:39,389 --> 00:18:41,289
break glass, it is bypass.

324
00:18:42,059 --> 00:18:43,949
Third is the policy
roll, rollout.

325
00:18:44,449 --> 00:18:47,139
We need to treat recovery
policy like code.

326
00:18:48,029 --> 00:18:51,809
Validate-- Which means that we
need to validate a new policy

327
00:18:51,839 --> 00:18:56,479
against retrained traces,
shadow both versions, then

328
00:18:56,769 --> 00:19:01,149
atom-- then atomically advance
the policy epoch roll a

329
00:19:01,149 --> 00:19:06,769
policy back only if no action
authorized exclusively by that

330
00:19:06,819 --> 00:19:11,479
policy has already crossed
a non-compensable boundary.

331
00:19:11,979 --> 00:19:15,129
So the principle under
all three is the same.

332
00:19:15,729 --> 00:19:19,109
Insufficient evidence is
never permission to resume

333
00:19:19,609 --> 00:19:22,799
So the next question that
we need to answer is what

334
00:19:22,799 --> 00:19:24,209
replaces the two numbers?

335
00:19:24,709 --> 00:19:26,619
And the answer is
nothing replaces them.

336
00:19:27,459 --> 00:19:32,149
RTO and RPO stay as
continuity objectives.

337
00:19:32,869 --> 00:19:38,309
The envelope adds five bounded
objectives set per workload

338
00:19:38,679 --> 00:19:40,599
and set-- and per key scope.

339
00:19:41,099 --> 00:19:45,529
How many events or how much
of a window may be absent

340
00:19:45,539 --> 00:19:47,099
from a published result?

341
00:19:47,599 --> 00:19:52,039
How many replayed effects may
reach a sync or a customer?

342
00:19:52,539 --> 00:19:56,519
How many older updates
may land after new ones?

343
00:19:57,069 --> 00:20:01,169
What is the maximum age
of restored state with

344
00:20:01,379 --> 00:20:05,359
authorization state bounded
separately and more tightly?

345
00:20:05,859 --> 00:20:08,839
And non-compensable
divergence, which for

346
00:20:08,859 --> 00:20:14,329
irreversible actions is zero,
not a weight, a prohibition.

347
00:20:14,829 --> 00:20:18,199
The value of writing these
down before an incident is

348
00:20:18,919 --> 00:20:23,349
that the trade-off between
availability and correctness

349
00:20:23,609 --> 00:20:28,279
becomes a policy decision
made calmly instead of

350
00:20:28,339 --> 00:20:31,809
an improvisation made
at three in the morning

351
00:20:32,309 --> 00:20:36,199
So what do auditors
and security reviewers

352
00:20:36,279 --> 00:20:39,549
actually ask for after
a recovery, right?

353
00:20:39,639 --> 00:20:42,939
They ask for four things,
and the envelope produces

354
00:20:42,959 --> 00:20:45,859
all four as a byproduct
of operating correctly.

355
00:20:46,789 --> 00:20:50,489
The first is the pre-execution
attestation, which is nothing

356
00:20:50,489 --> 00:20:55,089
but the evidence that was used
bound to one decision frontier

357
00:20:55,209 --> 00:20:59,839
and schema version, journaled
before the, plan ran.

358
00:21:00,339 --> 00:21:03,589
And then we have the
strategy rationale, which

359
00:21:03,589 --> 00:21:07,009
is the feasible set, every
rejected candidate, and the

360
00:21:07,029 --> 00:21:08,879
constraint that rejected it.

361
00:21:09,379 --> 00:21:13,939
Post-recovery validation
is another one where a

362
00:21:13,939 --> 00:21:18,129
verifier confirms the
selected envelope was not

363
00:21:18,159 --> 00:21:19,919
exceeded after execution.

364
00:21:20,819 --> 00:21:24,909
And finally, we have a
reproducibility, which is the

365
00:21:24,909 --> 00:21:29,529
same journaled evidence, the
policy version, and planner

366
00:21:29,529 --> 00:21:33,299
version give the auditor
the same plan you executed.

367
00:21:34,069 --> 00:21:36,499
So this is the difference
between telling a reviewer

368
00:21:36,889 --> 00:21:39,769
that the recovery was
safe and showing them.

369
00:21:40,269 --> 00:21:42,589
So three things
to take with you.

370
00:21:43,519 --> 00:21:47,089
The first is recovery is a
semantic planning problem,

371
00:21:47,389 --> 00:21:51,269
which means that five
bounded damage dimensions

372
00:21:51,769 --> 00:21:55,589
measured per key scope
and horizon supplement

373
00:21:55,609 --> 00:21:59,679
two scalars that hide the
trade-offs that matter Then

374
00:21:59,779 --> 00:22:03,729
safety before optimization,
where hard constraints,

375
00:22:03,959 --> 00:22:06,399
then Pareto, then policy.

376
00:22:06,859 --> 00:22:10,499
The fastest plan can lose,
and when it does, the

377
00:22:10,499 --> 00:22:11,639
reason is written down.

378
00:22:12,139 --> 00:22:14,549
And evidence, not inference.

379
00:22:15,049 --> 00:22:17,059
Unknown evidence
widens the bound.

380
00:22:17,539 --> 00:22:19,049
Epochs are fenced.

381
00:22:19,419 --> 00:22:23,239
The decision is journaled, and
an auditor can reproduce it.

382
00:22:23,739 --> 00:22:25,719
Restoring uptime is necessary.

383
00:22:26,329 --> 00:22:29,129
Restoring integrity
is non-negotiable

384
00:22:29,629 --> 00:22:32,329
So the ideas here build
on established work.

385
00:22:33,099 --> 00:22:36,279
Consistent asynchronous
snapshots and state management

386
00:22:36,279 --> 00:22:41,979
in Apache Flink, the DEBS
2024 benchmarking of fault

387
00:22:42,129 --> 00:22:46,719
recovery in stream processors,
data trace types, watermark

388
00:22:46,729 --> 00:22:51,639
semantics, Kafka's ordering
and exactly-once guarantees,

389
00:22:52,059 --> 00:22:54,769
and the approximate fault
tolerance and adaptive

390
00:22:54,809 --> 00:22:56,149
checkpointing lines of work.

391
00:22:56,869 --> 00:22:59,199
The envelope does not
replace any of them.

392
00:22:59,829 --> 00:23:02,599
It adds a decision
layer above them

393
00:23:03,099 --> 00:23:04,979
Finally, thank
you for watching.

394
00:23:05,339 --> 00:23:09,989
I hope this framework helps
you evaluate recovery not

395
00:23:09,989 --> 00:23:14,009
only by speed, but also by
correctness, security, and the

396
00:23:14,019 --> 00:23:16,159
evidence behind each decision.

397
00:23:16,659 --> 00:23:17,009
Thank you

